-- ============================================================================
-- DR11 ASSOCIATE DEEP SEED: Well Integrity & P&A, beginner tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: the two barrier envelopes. What an element is, which
-- envelope it serves, what its status claims, how an envelope's verdict
-- follows from its worst element, and what the resulting category obliges.
--
-- THE IDEA THE TIER IS BUILT ON IS EVIDENCE. An element is 'verified' only if
-- somebody tested it and the test is current. 'not-verified' is not a lesser
-- problem than 'degraded': it produces the IDENTICAL envelope verdict, the
-- identical category and the identical reason sentence, and the only thing
-- that distinguishes them is a line in the checks list telling you the remedy
-- is a TEST rather than an intervention. A barrier you cannot demonstrate is
-- not a barrier you can rely on.
--
-- TWO VOCABULARIES, DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT. An ELEMENT is verified, degraded,
-- failed or not-verified. An ENVELOPE is intact, degraded, failed or empty.
-- Two words are in BOTH lists, which is exactly what makes mixing them easy,
-- and mixing them is what this course caught the engine failing at.
--
-- SEATS ARE NOT ELEMENTS. An element serving BOTH envelopes is counted by each
-- of them, so adding the two counts gives SEATS. The gap between seats and
-- physical elements is exactly the number of common elements, and that gap is
-- the honest measure of how much independence a well actually has. The
-- two-barrier rule assumes two independent envelopes; a shared element is a
-- place where that assumption is simply untrue, which is why the engine flags
-- it and why it is acceptable only by explicit dispensation.
--
-- THE ENGINE DEFECT THIS TIER FOUND, AND IT FAILED IN THE ONLY DIRECTION THAT
-- MATTERS. wellCategory accepted any string and fell through to GREEN for
-- anything it did not recognise, so handing it an ELEMENT status returned a
-- clean bill of health for a well nobody had checked. An EMPTY primary
-- envelope read green too, on a well without flow potential, with a reason
-- naming a "Qualified barrier" that did not exist. Its sibling envelopeStatus
-- had always refused an unknown word. When two halves of one function disagree
-- about whether to trust their input, the trusting half is the bug. Both fixed
-- in petrolord-engines PR #105.
-- ============================================================================

